#!/usr/bin/env -S python3 -I
"""Merge a unit's branch into its target, main into an issue branch, or an issue branch into local main. Only the
PM runs this, and a plan for its own issue; there are no pull requests.

  pm-merge <issue> <unit> [--dry-run]      a unit into its target: main, or the issue branch feat/<issue>
  pm-merge <issue> --take-main [--dry-run] local main into the issue branch, in the issue's worktree
  pm-merge <issue> [--dry-run]             the issue branch into local main

The target of a unit is decided from git as pm-scope decides it (_pmscope.target_of): feat/<issue> for a branch
<feat|refactor|fix>/<issue>-<rest> when that local branch exists, else main. A unit whose target is main is merged
as described next, word for word as before the issue branch existed.

A unit into main. Refuses unless: the main checkout is on main with a clean tree; the unit's worktree has nothing uncommitted; the
unit's last pm-check run passed on the commit being merged; and rev passed the unit: the newest
review-impl-<unit>-r<k>.md in .pm/issue-<n>/ says verdict: pass. A test unit (t<n>) that is merged by itself has
no implementation review; its newest review-checks-<unit>-r<k>.md counts instead. (A test unit whose checks are
red by design never gets here: its pm-check does not pass, and it arrives through the unit stacked on it.)
A run that passed with a flaky step (failed once, passed on the second run) is accepted and named in the output.

Coverage. pm-merge decides the branch's paths against main again itself (the paths between its merge base with
main and its head), with the rules of this main checkout (_pmscope.py, as pm-scope), and refuses unless the
record's steps show, for every module those paths call for, that the module is absent or that each of its steps
passed or was flaky. A step recorded not-needed, failed or missing does not cover its module. What the record's
`scope` says is not trusted for this; only its steps are. Paths that cannot be read (say, a branch that shares no
commit with main) call for every module.
Services (issue 327). For a unit into main and for the issue branch into main, and only when the branch's paths
against main call for integration, it decides the branch's selection of services again itself (_pmscope.select of
the same paths, with the manifests of the merge base and of the branch's head, by the rules of this main
checkout; each manifest is the main manifest's rows, then each service's under tests/integration/studio/services/,
issue 402), and refuses unless the record's integration step that ran (passed or flaky) ran at least that: its
`services` is null (every service's checks), or the branch's selection is a set and every name of it is in the
step's list. The refusal names what the record ran and what the branch touches and says run pm-check again. A step
without `services` in a record whose `scope` has no `services` was written before this question and ran every
check: accepted. A step without it in a record whose `scope` has it is refused (the record does not say). A unit
into its issue branch (a deferred step), an integration module that is absent, or paths that do not call for
integration ask nothing. What main gained since the branch left it is judged below.
The main it merges into (issue 520, as a pull request is merged). Into main, what main gained since the branch
left it refuses nothing, whatever modules it calls for: a branch whose record passes on its head and covers its own
paths is merged when git merges it with no conflict, a fast-forward or a clean merge commit, with no second check.
When main has moved, the output says the branch is behind main, by how many paths and what they call for (and
which of them the branch's own paths call for too), and the merge's line in .pm/run/events.log ends with
`behind main by <n> paths`; the run of .github/workflows/main.yml on the push is the check of the merged result.
When git stops on a conflict, nothing is merged and the output names the conflicting paths and the lead's step:
the lead merges main into its branch, the conflict is resolved by the unit that owns the files, the check runs
again on the new head, then rerun. A branch that took main has a new head, so the record of its old head is
refused as any record of another commit is.
A record without `scope` and without `module` on its steps was written by the pm-check before issue 52, which
passed only after running every module: it is judged as before, and none of these questions is asked of it.
Merges with --no-ff so every unit is one merge commit on main. On a conflict the merge is aborted and main is left
as it was: the unit's lead (the harness-engineer of a harness issue, else its worker) merges main into its branch,
resolves the conflict, checks again, then rerun.
After the merge commit it runs `pm-emulator-dir rm --worktree` for the unit's worktree and for each registered
worktree of the issue it is stacked on (the chain of base branches down to main), and prints their lines: the
emulator port folders those units leave under ~/.aws/emulator. A refusal or a failure there does not change the
merge or the exit code. A refused merge, --dry-run and an aborted merge remove nothing.

A unit into its issue branch. Merged with --no-ff into feat/<issue> in the issue's worktree (the registry entry
with unit "issue"); main and the main checkout are not touched or asked. Refuses unless: the issue's worktree is
registered, on feat/<issue> and clean; the unit's worktree is clean; the unit's last pm-check run passed on the
commit being merged; its steps cover every module the unit's own paths against feat/<issue> call for, where an
integration step recorded "deferred" (the fast check) also covers integration, here only; feat/<issue> holds what
the unit was cut from, or what it gained since calls for no module but integration that the unit's paths call
for (otherwise: merge feat/<issue> into the unit's branch, pm-check, rerun); and the review rule above holds. On a
conflict the merge is aborted and the issue branch is left as it was. After the merge it removes the emulator
port folders of the unit and of the units it is stacked on, not the issue's worktree's. A unit is merged into its
issue branch as many times as it is fixed.
--take-main. Merges local main into feat/<issue> in the issue's worktree, which must be registered, on its branch
and clean. When main is already an ancestor it says so and changes nothing. On a conflict it aborts, leaves the
issue branch as it was and names the conflicting paths: the unit that owns them merges main into its branch.
The issue branch into main. Merged with --no-ff in the main checkout. Refuses unless: the main checkout is on main
and clean; the issue's worktree is on feat/<issue> and clean; the branch of every registered unit of the issue is
an ancestor of feat/<issue>; the review rule holds for each of them; the issue's worktree's last pm-check run
(the whole check) passed on the head of feat/<issue> and covers every module the branch's paths against main call
for, a "deferred" step covering nothing. What main gained is judged as for a unit into main: a clean merge goes in,
behind or not; a conflict is refused, main left as it was, and the output says: pm-merge <issue> --take-main, the
unit that owns each conflicting path merges main into its branch and resolves it, is merged into feat/<issue>
again, the whole check, rerun. After the merge it removes the emulator port folders of the
issue's worktree and of every unit's, and names the units and the record it merged on.
Who may run it (issue 478). The pm runs every form. A plan runs `pm-merge <its issue> <unit>` for a unit whose target
is feat/<its issue>, and `pm-merge <its issue> --take-main`; a unit into main and the issue branch into main are the
pm's. Every other pane is refused.
The lead of the issue, the mark `lead` that pm-brief writes into .pm/issue-<n>/brief.json, changes the review rule:
`plan`: a unit goes into feat/<issue> on its fast check alone (rev reviews the whole at the end), and feat/<issue>
goes into main only when the newest review-impl-r<k>.md (the whole, stage `impl`) says verdict: pass.
`harness-engineer`: no review file is asked; the pm that runs the merge has judged the change, and the output says so.
A harness-engineer's part closes its issue (issue 504): a unit into main of such an issue is refused while a file under
issues/things-that-do-not-work/ holds `Issue-Id: <issue>` at the branch's head, unless brief.json records the unit's
part `not-last` (pm-brief harness-engineer --not-last); a unit with no record is a last part. The refusal says to
run pm-issue close on the branch, pm-check, then rerun.
Without the mark (an issue begun before issue 478) the per-unit rule above holds, word for word.
`pm-merge <issue> issue` is refused: the issue branch is not a unit. --dry-run checks every condition of its form,
lists every problem, and merges nothing; into main it also asks git merge-tree --write-tree, which touches no
worktree, whether git merges the branch clean: a conflict is a problem naming its paths and the lead's step, and a
merge-tree that cannot answer is said and is no problem. The new forms time their merges out after 120 s (the merge is then
aborted) and every read of git after 30 s.
Never pushes; the PM pushes main when an issue is finished (pm skill, flow step 3).
"""
import argparse
import json
import os
import pathlib
import re
import subprocess
import sys

sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
from _pmlib import BIN, PM, ROOT, WORKTREES, guard, load, log
from _pmscope import (MODULES, Unreadable, between, decide, manifest_at, merge_base, run_git, select,
                      target_of)
from _pmscope import git as read_git

EMULATOR_DIR = "pm-emulator-dir: "
ISSUE = "issue"  # the registry's unit of an issue branch (pm-worktree new <issue>)
MERGE_TIMEOUT = 120  # seconds a merge of a new form may take before it is aborted
ABORT_TIMEOUT = 30


def git(*args, cwd=ROOT):
    return subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(cwd), *args], capture_output=True, text=True)


def remove_emulator_dir(wt):
    """Run pm-emulator-dir for one worktree and pass on its lines; nothing it does changes the merge."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run([str(BIN / "pm-emulator-dir"), "rm", "--worktree", wt["path"]], capture_output=True,
                           text=True, timeout=60)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        print(f"{EMULATOR_DIR}did not run for {wt['name']}: timeout after 60 s")
        return
    except OSError as e:
        print(f"{EMULATOR_DIR}did not run for {wt['name']}: {type(e).__name__}")
        return
    lines = [ln for ln in r.stdout.splitlines() if ln.startswith(EMULATOR_DIR)]
    print("\n".join(lines) if lines else f"{EMULATOR_DIR}no line for {wt['name']} (exit {r.returncode})")


def earlier(last):
    """A record of the pm-check before issue 52: no scope and no step with its module."""
    return "scope" not in last and not any("module" in s for s in last.get("steps") or [])


def covered(statuses):
    return bool(statuses) and (all(s == "absent" for s in statuses) or all(s in ("passed", "flaky") for s in statuses))


def plural(n):
    return f"{n} path" if n == 1 else f"{n} paths"


def services_problems(last, need):
    """Whether the record's integration step ran the checks of the services the branch touches (`need`, the
    services object of its paths). Asks nothing of a step that did not run (absent, failed, not-needed)."""
    ran = []
    for s in last.get("steps") or []:
        if s.get("module") != "integration" or s.get("status") not in ("passed", "flaky"):
            continue
        if "services" in s:
            v = s["services"]
        elif "services" not in (last.get("scope") or {}):
            v = None  # a record from before the question, which ran every check
        else:
            return ["the pm-check record does not say which services' checks ran; run pm-check again"]
        if v is not None and not (isinstance(v, list) and all(isinstance(n, str) for n in v)):
            return ["the pm-check record's services cannot be read; run pm-check again"]
        ran.append(v)
    if not ran or any(v is None for v in ran):
        return []
    have = sorted({n for v in ran for n in v})
    if not need["all"] and all(n in have for n in need["names"]):
        return []
    touch = f"every service: {need['why']}" if need["all"] else ", ".join(need["names"])
    return [f"the pm-check record ran the checks of {', '.join(have) or 'no service'}, and this branch's changes "
            f"touch {touch}; run pm-check again"]


def branch_services(base, head, paths):
    """The selection of the branch's paths, with the manifests of the merge base and the head; a manifest that
    cannot be read is passed as None, which select answers with every service where it matters."""
    def at(commit):
        try:
            return manifest_at(ROOT, commit)
        except Unreadable:
            return None
    return select(paths, at(head), at(base))


def judge(branch, head, last, target="main", defer=False, remedy=None):
    """Coverage (R7) and the target the branch merges into (R8), decided from git with this checkout's rules.
    `target` is main or an issue branch; `defer` (a unit into its issue branch only) lets a deferred integration
    step cover integration and leaves integration out of the modules both sides call for. `remedy` is what the
    behind problem tells the PM to do. Into main (issue 520) what main gained since the branch left it refuses
    nothing: git decides at the merge, a conflict refuses it, and the note says the branch is behind. Into an issue
    branch it refuses as before when both sides call for a module. Returns (problems, note, the number of paths
    the target gained, or None when it gained none or they cannot be read)."""
    tip = base = None
    try:
        tip = read_git(ROOT, "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", f"refs/heads/{target}^{{commit}}").strip()
        base = merge_base(ROOT, head, tip, target)
        paths = between(ROOT, base, head)
        own = decide(paths)
        need = None if defer or "integration" not in own else branch_services(base, head, paths)
    except Unreadable as e:
        own = {m: [f"the branch's changes cannot be read: {e}"] for m in MODULES}
        need = None if defer else dict(all=True, names=[], why=f"the branch's changes cannot be read: {e}")
    problems = []
    statuses = {}
    for s in last.get("steps") or []:
        statuses.setdefault(s.get("module"), []).append(s.get("status"))

    def holds(m):
        st = statuses.get(m)
        return covered(st) or (defer and m == "integration" and bool(st) and all(s == "deferred" for s in st))

    missing = [m for m in MODULES if m in own and not holds(m)]
    if missing:
        problems.append(f"the pm-check record does not cover {', '.join(missing)}, which this branch's changes call "
                        f"for ({own[missing[0]][0]}); run pm-check again")
    if need is not None:
        problems += services_problems(last, need)
    if base is not None and base == tip:
        return problems, None, None
    gained = None
    try:
        if base is None:
            raise Unreadable(f"the branch's merge base with {target} cannot be read")
        gained = sorted(set(between(ROOT, base, tip)))
        theirs = decide(gained)
    except Unreadable as e:
        theirs = {m: [str(e)] for m in MODULES}
    both = [m for m in MODULES if m in own and m in theirs and not (defer and m == "integration")]
    if target == "main":
        if gained is None:
            return problems, (f"behind main; what main gained since this branch left it cannot be read; git decides "
                              f"at the merge, and the run on GitHub Actions checks the merged result"), None
        mods = ", ".join(theirs) or "nothing"
        if both:
            return problems, (f"behind main by {plural(len(gained))} that call for {mods}; this branch's changes call "
                              f"for {', '.join(both)} too; merged on its own record when git merges it clean, not "
                              f"checked with them: the run on GitHub Actions checks the merged result"), len(gained)
        return problems, (f"behind main by {plural(len(gained))} that call for {mods}; "
                          f"this branch's changes call for none of them"), len(gained)
    if both:
        if gained is None:
            what = (f"what {target} gained since this branch left it cannot be read ({theirs[both[0]][0]}), so both "
                    f"call for {', '.join(both)}")
        else:
            eg = ", ".join(f"{m}: {next(p for p in gained if m in decide([p]))}" for m in both)
            what = f"{target} gained {plural(len(gained))} since this branch left it; both call for {', '.join(both)} (e.g. {eg})"
        problems.append(f"{what}; {remedy or f'merge {target} into {branch} and run pm-check again'}")
        return problems, None, None
    note = (f"behind {target} by {plural(len(gained))} that call for {', '.join(theirs) or 'nothing'}; "
            f"this branch's changes call for none of them")
    return problems, note, len(gained)


def stack(wts, wt):
    """The unit's entry, then the entry of the same issue whose branch is its base, down to main or to the issue
    branch, whose own entry is not in the chain."""
    chain, seen = [], set()
    while wt is not None and wt.get("unit") != ISSUE and wt["branch"] not in seen:
        chain.append(wt)
        seen.add(wt["branch"])
        base = wt.get("base")
        wt = None if base in (None, "main") else next(
            (w for w in wts if w.get("issue") == chain[0]["issue"] and w.get("branch") == base), None)
    return chain


def review(issue, unit):
    """The review rule: the newest review-impl-<unit>-r<k>.md (for a test unit without one, its newest
    review-checks-<unit>-r<k>.md) says verdict: pass. Returns (problems, the newest review file or None)."""
    def rounds(stage):
        return sorted((PM / f"issue-{issue}").glob(f"review-{stage}-{unit}-r*.md"),
                      key=lambda f: int(re.search(r"-r(\d+)\.md$", f.name).group(1)))

    reviews = rounds("impl")
    if not reviews and unit.startswith("t"):
        reviews = rounds("checks")
    if not reviews:
        want = f"review-impl-{unit}-r<k>.md" + (f" or review-checks-{unit}-r<k>.md" if unit.startswith("t") else "")
        return [f"no {want}; rev has not reviewed this unit"], None
    if not re.search(r"^verdict:\s*pass\s*$", reviews[-1].read_text(errors="replace")[:400], re.M):
        return [f"{reviews[-1].name} is not verdict: pass"], reviews[-1]
    return [], reviews[-1]


def lead(issue):
    """`plan`, `harness-engineer`, or None for an issue begun before issue 478 (pm-brief writes the mark)."""
    return load(PM / f"issue-{issue}" / "brief.json", {}).get("lead")


def open_issue_files(issue, ref):
    """The files under issues/things-that-do-not-work/ that hold `Issue-Id: <issue>` at ref."""
    r = git("grep", "-l", "-E", f"^Issue-Id: {int(issue)}$", ref, "--", "issues/things-that-do-not-work/")
    return [ln.split(":", 1)[1] for ln in r.stdout.splitlines() if ":" in ln]


def close_problems(issue, unit, branch, head):
    """A harness-engineer's last part must close its issue on its branch (issue 504); [] for any other merge."""
    if lead(issue) != "harness-engineer":
        return []
    parts = load(PM / f"issue-{issue}" / "brief.json", {}).get("parts") or {}
    if parts.get(unit) == "not-last":
        return []
    found = open_issue_files(issue, head)
    if not found:
        return []
    return [f"the issue's file is still open on {branch}: {found[0]}; this part closes the issue: on the branch run "
            f"pm-issue close {issue} --checks \"<ids>\" --in <its worktree>, commit, pm-check, then rerun (a part that "
            f"does not close it: pm-brief harness-engineer {issue} {unit} --not-last)"]


def unit_review(issue, unit):
    """(problems, what the merge was judged on) for one unit, by the issue's lead."""
    led = lead(issue)
    if led == "harness-engineer":
        return [], "the pm's own judgement (harness issue)"
    if led == "plan":
        return [], "its fast check (rev reviews the whole at the end)"
    found, reviewed = review(issue, unit)
    return found, (reviewed.name if reviewed else None)


def whole_review(issue):
    """(problems, what it was judged on) for the issue branch of a plan-led issue: the newest review-impl-r<k>.md."""
    reviews = sorted((PM / f"issue-{issue}").glob("review-impl-r*.md"),
                     key=lambda f: int(re.search(r"-r(\d+)\.md$", f.name).group(1)))
    if not reviews:
        return ["no review-impl-r<k>.md; rev has not reviewed the whole issue branch"], None
    if not re.search(r"^verdict:\s*pass\s*$", reviews[-1].read_text(errors="replace")[:400], re.M):
        return [f"{reviews[-1].name} is not verdict: pass"], reviews[-1].name
    return [], reviews[-1].name


def record_problems(last, head):
    """Today's three questions of a record: it exists, it is of the head, it passed."""
    if not last:
        return ["no pm-check run recorded for this worktree"]
    if last.get("commit") != head:
        return [f"pm-check ran on {str(last.get('commit'))[:9]}, the branch is at {head[:9]}; run pm-check again"]
    if not last.get("passed"):
        return [f"pm-check failed: {', '.join(last.get('failed', []))}"]
    return []


def issue_entry(wts, issue):
    """The registry entry of the issue's branch (unit "issue"), or None."""
    return next((w for w in wts if w.get("issue") == str(issue) and w.get("unit") == ISSUE), None)


def units(wts, issue):
    """The registry entries of the issue's units: every entry of the issue but the issue branch's own."""
    return [w for w in wts if w.get("issue") == str(issue) and w.get("unit") != ISSUE]


def ancestor(a, b):
    """Whether commit `a` is an ancestor of `b` in the main checkout; raises Unreadable when git cannot say."""
    r = run_git(ROOT, "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", a, b)
    if r.returncode not in (0, 1):
        raise Unreadable(f"git merge-base --is-ancestor exited {r.returncode}")
    return r.returncode == 0


def tip(ref):
    """The commit of a local branch; raises Unreadable when it does not exist."""
    return read_git(ROOT, "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", f"refs/heads/{ref}^{{commit}}").strip()


def clean(path, what=None):
    """[] when the worktree at `path` has nothing uncommitted, else the problem; unreadable is a problem too."""
    what = what or path.name
    try:
        return [f"{what} has uncommitted changes"] if read_git(path, "status", "--porcelain").strip() else []
    except Unreadable as e:
        return [f"{what} cannot be read: {e}"]


def issue_checkout(entry, branch):
    """The problems of the issue's worktree: registered, on its branch, clean."""
    if entry is None:
        return [f"{branch} exists but the issue's worktree is not registered"]
    path = pathlib.Path(entry["path"])
    try:
        r = run_git(path, "symbolic-ref", "--quiet", "--short", "HEAD")
    except Unreadable as e:
        return [f"{path.name} cannot be read: {e}"]
    on = r.stdout.strip() if r.returncode == 0 else None
    if r.returncode not in (0, 1):
        return [f"{path.name} cannot be read: git symbolic-ref exited {r.returncode}"]
    problems = [] if on == branch else [f"{path.name} is not on {branch} ({on or 'a detached head'})"]
    return problems + clean(path)


def merge(cwd, ref, message, dest, remedy=None):
    """`git merge --no-ff` in `cwd`, bounded; on a conflict or a timeout the merge is aborted and the process exits
    non-zero, naming `dest`, the conflicting paths and, on a conflict, `remedy`. Returns the new head."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(cwd), "merge", "--no-ff", "-m", message, ref], capture_output=True,
                           text=True, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=MERGE_TIMEOUT)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        sys.exit(f"merge did not finish in {MERGE_TIMEOUT} s; {left(cwd, dest)}")
    if r.returncode != 0:
        try:
            conflicts = [x for x in read_git(cwd, "diff", "--name-only", "--diff-filter=U", "-z").split("\0") if x]
        except Unreadable:
            conflicts = []
        what = f"merge conflict in {plural(len(conflicts))}" if conflicts else f"merge failed (exit {r.returncode})"
        named = f": {', '.join(conflicts)}" if conflicts else ""
        then = f"\n{remedy}" if remedy and conflicts else ""
        sys.exit(f"{what}; {left(cwd, dest)}{named}{then}\n{(r.stdout + r.stderr).strip()[-600:]}")
    return read_git(cwd, "rev-parse", "HEAD").strip()


def left(cwd, dest):
    """Abort the merge in `cwd`; say whether `dest` was left unchanged."""
    try:
        ok = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(cwd), "merge", "--abort"], capture_output=True, text=True,
                            stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=ABORT_TIMEOUT).returncode == 0
    except (subprocess.TimeoutExpired, OSError):
        ok = False
    return f"{dest} left unchanged" if ok else f"{dest} may hold an unfinished merge: run git merge --abort in {cwd}"


def flaky_note(last):
    return f"; flaky in its check run: {', '.join(last['flaky'])} (file the flake, see .pm/run/flakes.jsonl)" if last.get("flaky") else ""


def predict(branch, remedy):
    """--dry-run of a merge into main (issue 520): whether git merges `branch` into main clean, asked of
    `git merge-tree --write-tree`, which touches no worktree. Returns (problems, line): a conflict is a problem
    naming its paths and `remedy`; a merge-tree that cannot answer is a line, not a problem."""
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(ROOT), "merge-tree", "--write-tree", "--name-only", "--no-messages",
                            "refs/heads/main", f"refs/heads/{branch}"], capture_output=True, text=True,
                           stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=MERGE_TIMEOUT)
    except (subprocess.TimeoutExpired, OSError) as e:
        return [], f"whether git merges it clean cannot be told: git merge-tree did not run ({type(e).__name__})"
    if r.returncode == 0:
        return [], "git merges it into main clean"
    if r.returncode == 1:
        paths = [x for x in r.stdout.splitlines()[1:] if x]
        return [f"git would stop on a merge conflict in {plural(len(paths))}: {', '.join(paths)}; {remedy}"], None
    return [], f"whether git merges it clean cannot be told: git merge-tree exited {r.returncode}"


def behind_log(count):
    """The words the merge's line in events.log adds when the branch was behind main (issue 520)."""
    return f" behind main by {plural(count)}" if count else ""


def unit_remedy(issue, branch):
    """What a conflict of a unit into main asks: its lead merges main into the branch (issue 520)."""
    who = "the harness-engineer" if lead(issue) == "harness-engineer" else "its worker"
    return (f"{who} merges main into {branch} and resolves the conflict, runs pm-check, then rerun "
            f"pm-merge {issue} <unit>")


def whole_remedy(issue):
    """What a conflict of the issue branch into main asks of its plan (issue 520)."""
    return (f"the plan runs pm-merge {issue} --take-main, the unit that owns each path merges main into its branch "
            f"and resolves it, is merged into feat/{issue} again, then the whole check, then rerun pm-merge {issue}")


def main():
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__, formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    p.add_argument("issue"); p.add_argument("unit", nargs="?")
    p.add_argument("--take-main", action="store_true", help="merge local main into the issue branch")
    p.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true")
    a = p.parse_args()
    if a.take_main and a.unit is not None:
        p.error("--take-main takes no unit")
    if a.unit == ISSUE:
        sys.exit(f"{ISSUE} is not a unit; the issue branch is merged with pm-merge {a.issue}")
    asker = guard(f"pm-merge {a.issue}", issue=a.issue, plan_may=True)
    if asker and a.unit is None and not a.take_main:
        sys.exit(f"refused: the issue branch goes into main with pm-merge {a.issue}, the pm's; {asker['name']} may not")
    wts = load(WORKTREES, [])
    if a.take_main:
        return take_main(a, wts)
    if a.unit is None:
        return into_main_whole(a, wts)
    wt = next((w for w in wts if w["issue"] == str(a.issue) and w["unit"] == a.unit), None)
    if not wt:
        sys.exit(f"no registered worktree for issue {a.issue} unit {a.unit}")
    try:
        target = target_of(ROOT, wt["branch"])
    except Unreadable as e:
        sys.exit(f"cannot merge:\n- the target of {wt['branch']} cannot be read: {e}")
    if target == "main":
        if asker:
            sys.exit(f"refused: {wt['branch']} would go into main, which is the pm's; {asker['name']} merges into feat/{a.issue}")
        return into_main(a, wts, wt)
    return into_issue_branch(a, wts, wt, target)


def into_main(a, wts, wt):
    """A unit whose target is main: as before the issue branch."""
    branch, path = wt["branch"], pathlib.Path(wt["path"])
    problems = []
    if git("rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD").stdout.strip() != "main":
        problems.append("the main checkout is not on main")
    if git("status", "--porcelain").stdout.strip():
        problems.append("the main checkout has uncommitted changes")
    if git("status", "--porcelain", cwd=path).stdout.strip():
        problems.append(f"{path.name} has uncommitted changes")
    head = git("rev-parse", branch).stdout.strip()
    last = load(path / "output" / "checks" / "last.json", {})
    if not last:
        problems.append("no pm-check run recorded for this worktree")
    elif last.get("commit") != head:
        problems.append(f"pm-check ran on {str(last.get('commit'))[:9]}, the branch is at {head[:9]}; run pm-check again")
    elif not last.get("passed"):
        problems.append(f"pm-check failed: {', '.join(last.get('failed', []))}")
    note = count = None
    if last and last.get("commit") == head and last.get("passed") and not earlier(last):
        found, note, count = judge(branch, head, last)
        problems += found
    found, reviewed = unit_review(a.issue, a.unit)
    problems += found
    if head:
        problems += close_problems(a.issue, a.unit, branch, head)
    told = None
    if a.dry_run and head:
        more, told = predict(branch, unit_remedy(a.issue, branch))
        problems += more
    if problems:
        sys.exit("cannot merge:\n- " + "\n- ".join(problems))
    if note:
        print(note)
    if a.dry_run:
        if told:
            print(told)
        print(f"ok to merge {branch} ({head[:9]}) into main")
        return
    r = git("merge", "--no-ff", "-m", f"merge: #{a.issue} {a.unit} ({branch})", branch)
    if r.returncode != 0:
        conflicts = [x for x in git("diff", "--name-only", "--diff-filter=U", "-z").stdout.split("\0") if x]
        git("merge", "--abort")
        named = f" in {plural(len(conflicts))}: {', '.join(conflicts)}" if conflicts else ""
        sys.exit(f"merge conflict{named}; main left unchanged. {unit_remedy(a.issue, branch)}."
                 f"\n{r.stdout.strip()[-600:]}")
    new = git("rev-parse", "HEAD").stdout.strip()
    log(f"merge {a.issue} {a.unit} {branch} -> main {new[:9]}{behind_log(count)}")
    flaky = f"; flaky in its check run: {', '.join(last['flaky'])} (file the flake, see .pm/run/flakes.jsonl)" if last.get("flaky") else ""
    print(f"merged {branch} into main at {new[:9]} (reviewed: {reviewed}){flaky}; units stacked on it must merge main into their branch")
    for w in stack(wts, wt):
        remove_emulator_dir(w)


def into_issue_branch(a, wts, wt, target):
    """A unit into its issue branch, in the issue's worktree (R4.2)."""
    branch, path = wt["branch"], pathlib.Path(wt["path"])
    entry = next((w for w in wts if w.get("unit") == ISSUE and w.get("branch") == target), None)
    problems = issue_checkout(entry, target) + clean(path)
    try:
        head = tip(branch)
    except Unreadable as e:
        sys.exit(f"cannot merge:\n- {branch} cannot be read: {e}")
    last = load(path / "output" / "checks" / "last.json", {})
    found = record_problems(last, head)
    problems += found
    note = None
    if not found:
        more, note, _ = judge(branch, head, last, target, defer=True)
        problems += more
    more, reviewed = unit_review(a.issue, a.unit)
    problems += more
    if problems:
        sys.exit("cannot merge:\n- " + "\n- ".join(problems))
    if note:
        print(note)
    if a.dry_run:
        print(f"ok to merge {branch} ({head[:9]}) into {target}")
        return
    new = merge(entry["path"], branch, f"merge: #{a.issue} {a.unit} ({branch}) into {target}", target)
    log(f"merge {a.issue} {a.unit} {branch} -> {target} {new[:9]}")
    print(f"merged {branch} into {target} at {new[:9]} (reviewed: {reviewed}){flaky_note(last)}; "
          f"units cut from {target} must merge it into their branch")
    for w in stack(wts, wt):
        remove_emulator_dir(w)


def take_main(a, wts):
    """Local main into the issue branch, in the issue's worktree (R4.4)."""
    branch = f"feat/{a.issue}"
    entry = issue_entry(wts, a.issue)
    if entry is None:
        sys.exit(f"cannot merge:\n- issue {a.issue} has no registered issue branch (pm-worktree new {a.issue})")
    problems = issue_checkout(entry, branch)
    try:
        main_at, held = tip("main"), None
        if not problems:
            held = ancestor(main_at, tip(branch))
    except Unreadable as e:
        problems.append(f"the branches cannot be read: {e}")
    if problems:
        sys.exit("cannot merge:\n- " + "\n- ".join(problems))
    if held:
        print(f"{branch} already holds main ({main_at[:9]})")
        return
    if a.dry_run:
        print(f"would merge main ({main_at[:9]}) into {branch}")
        return
    new = merge(entry["path"], main_at, f"merge: main into {branch} (#{a.issue})", branch)
    log(f"merge main {main_at[:9]} -> {branch} {new[:9]}")
    print(f"merged main ({main_at[:9]}) into {branch} at {new[:9]}; run the whole check: pm-check {entry['path']}")


def into_main_whole(a, wts):
    """The issue branch into local main, on the whole check (R4.5)."""
    branch = f"feat/{a.issue}"
    entry = issue_entry(wts, a.issue)
    if entry is None:
        sys.exit(f"cannot merge:\n- issue {a.issue} has no registered issue branch; merge its units with "
                 f"pm-merge {a.issue} <unit>")
    path = pathlib.Path(entry["path"])
    problems = []
    try:
        if read_git(ROOT, "rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD").strip() != "main":
            problems.append("the main checkout is not on main")
    except Unreadable as e:
        problems.append(f"the main checkout cannot be read: {e}")
    problems += clean(ROOT, "the main checkout")
    problems += issue_checkout(entry, branch)
    try:
        head = tip(branch)
    except Unreadable as e:
        sys.exit(f"cannot merge:\n- {branch} cannot be read: {e}")
    mine = units(wts, a.issue)
    try:
        out = [w["branch"] for w in mine if not ancestor(w["branch"], head)]
        if out:
            problems.append(f"not in {branch}: {', '.join(out)}; merge them with pm-merge {a.issue} <unit>")
    except Unreadable as e:
        problems.append(f"the units' branches cannot be read: {e}")
    record = path / "output" / "checks" / "last.json"
    last = load(record, {})
    found = record_problems(last, head)
    problems += found
    note = count = None
    if not found:
        more, note, count = judge(branch, head, last, "main")
        problems += more
    judged = None
    if lead(a.issue) == "plan":
        more, judged = whole_review(a.issue)
        problems += more
    else:
        for w in mine:
            more, judged = unit_review(a.issue, w["unit"])
            problems += more
    told = None
    if a.dry_run:
        more, told = predict(branch, whole_remedy(a.issue))
        problems += more
    if problems:
        sys.exit("cannot merge:\n- " + "\n- ".join(problems))
    if note:
        print(note)
    if a.dry_run:
        if told:
            print(told)
        print(f"ok to merge {branch} ({head[:9]}) into main")
        return
    new = merge(ROOT, branch, f"merge: #{a.issue} ({branch})", "main", whole_remedy(a.issue))
    log(f"merge {a.issue} {branch} -> main {new[:9]}{behind_log(count)}")
    names = ", ".join(w["unit"] for w in mine) or "none"
    reviewed = f"; reviewed: {judged}" if lead(a.issue) else ""
    print(f"merged {branch} into main at {new[:9]} (units: {names}; whole check: {record}{reviewed}){flaky_note(last)}")
    for w in [entry, *mine]:
        remove_emulator_dir(w)


main()
